A1. German waiter's first name spelled backwards. ~ OTTO
A5. Wild, Alp-jumping goat. ~ IBEX
A9. Darwin and Bryan disagree about these members of a branch of the tree of evolution. ~ BABOONS
A12. Neckties (highbrow). ~ CRAVATS
A15. Flapper food. ~ CHOCOLATES
A17. A kind of flower (pl.). ~ CARNATIONS
A19. Things you wouldn't walk a mile for if you had a chance to ride. ~ CAMELS
A20. Deficiency of blood (as in lounge-lizards and turnips). ~ ANEMIA
A21. Noises caused by sleeping with the cut-out open. ~ SNORES
A23. Neat but not gaudy. ~ TIDY
A26. K. K. K. ~ CLAN
A28. A couple o' 37 horizontals. ~ RELICS
A31. A Scotchman does this with his money. ~ HOLDS
A32. What you do on the wrong horse. ~ BET
A33. She started all the trouble. ~ EVE
A35. Something you do when you read 80 horizontal. (Phonetic.) ~ LAF
A37. A survival. ~ RELIC
A38. What is the first syllable of Isadore's name? Is, is it? Yes, it is is. ~ IS
A39. Negative conjunction. (Ardent follower of "neither.") ~ NOR
A40. Young sheik. ~ LAD
A41. Wounded ship's call for succor. ~ SOS
A43. Where peaches and outfielders grow (abr.). ~ GA
A44. What Daniel Boone hung his cap on. ~ PEG
A46. The King of Beasties. ~ LEO
A47. There's usually one in every college town. ~ COLLEGE
A51. Mythical crossword bird. ~ ROC
A53. Two of these make a horse laugh. ~ HAR
A54. What you must do to become a revolutionist. ~ REVOLT
A56. Sixty minutes (abr.). ~ HR
A57. The beginning of asphyxiation. ~ AS
A58. Sock doctor. (Remember a sock on the foot is worth two on the jaw!) ~ DARNER
A60. Very much like metal. Oh, very much indeed! ~ METALLIC
A61. What 125,000,000 people think that prohibition enforcement is. ~ DAYDREAM
A62. Chemical symbol for the chief constituent of Yankee five-cent piece. (Ask your druggist. He probably won't know either.) ~ NI
A63. French for "classy shack." ~ CHATEAU
A64. English for "hat." ~ AT
A65. Chinese, Rotarys, Elks and Kiwanis clubs. ~ TONGS
A67. When these happen they call the wagon. ~ RIOTS
A69. Eggnog, without the egg. ~ NOG
A70. Subthig thad megs your dose stobbed up. ~ ADENOID
A73. To contend. ~ VIE
A74. What the ocean is after a rain. ~ WET
A76. Home of an ancient flapper queen. ~ SHEBA
A77. Slim, sleek, slippery, slender fish. ~ EEL
A78. Served with fried shad. ~ ROE
A79. What raspberry jam comes in. ~ JAR
A80. What you're looking at. ~ JUDGE
A84. A girl's name (kinda fancy). ~ ROSALIE
A85. Favorite parking place for cinders. ~ EYE

D1. Inoffensive wind instruments (not saxaphones). ~ OBOES
D2. Bridge tax (No, Dora, don't ask your dentist!) ~ TOLL
D3. Maker of rafts for poached eggs. ~ TOASTER
D4. You might as well stay in the "Land of the Free" as go to this Canadian Province (abr.). ~ ONT
D5. Irish Railroad (abr.). Don't look this up! ~ IRR
D6. Yes, we still haven't any. ~ BANANAS
D7. A man's name. ~ EVAN
D8. Manufacturer of Xats. (Look in the booktionary if you don't believe it.) ~ XATER
D9. What directors sit on (pl.) ~ BOARDS
D10. Perfection (pl.). ~ ACMES
D11. Something you put in the ground for your neighbor's chickens to dig up. ~ SEED
D12. What your poor relations do when it's time for dinner. ~ CALL
D13. Glad to see you, old ——! ~ TIMER
D14. The kind of clothes you send to the laundry to get a demonstration of the law of diminishing returns. ~ SOILED
D16. High cost of living (abr.). ~ HCOL
D18. Carpenter's pants button. ~ NAIL
D21. What took Emma Goldman to her dear Russia. ~ SHIP
D22. A bump on the beezer (so the book says). ~ NOSER
D24. A pronoun. ~ IT
D25. What you let out just after the dentist said it wouldn't hurt. ~ YELL
D26. To yield or transfer. ~ CEDE
D27. Jack Keefe's pal. (You know me.) ~ AL
D29. When you finish this you are at the end of your rope. ~ CIGAR
D30. What vaccinations leave. ~ SCAR
D32. Medium between Broadway and the spirit world. ~ BOOTLEGGER
D34. February 14. ~ VALENTINESDAY
D36. Lizzie tamer. (Two words.) ~ FORDDRIVER
D39. National Electric Light Association (abr.). ~ NELA
D42. What stocks do after you sell 'em. ~ SOAR
D45. Anything rare and perfect. (This puzzle frinstance.) ~ GEM
D46. Keeps the chaps away from the skin you love to touch. ~ LOTION
D47. Spiffy. ~ CHIC
D48. "Say it with flowers," but if you say it with these you'd better limit your conversation. ~ ORCHIDS
D49. A shepherd caste of India. (Look under G in the big dictionary.) ~ GADARIA
D50. The fellow who sold his birthright. ~ ESAU
D52. To make or produce. ~ CREATE
D53. Senior partner of Hem & Haw. ~ HEM
D55. An outlet for hot air. ~ VENT
D59. The Giants were the best of these (abr.). ~ NATS
D66. When to do it. ~ NOW
D68. It's poured on troubled waters. (Ask your Congressman!) ~ OIL
D71. An interrogative that goes with an ear trumpet. ~ EH
D72. Oh, boy! (Abr.) ~ OB
D75. Kitty's boy friend. ~ TOM
D77. One of the things Mark Anthony asked to borrow. ~ EAR
D80. Man's name (abr.). ~ JO
D81. Kind of car (good as far as it goes). ~ USED
D82. Kind of singing club (not spree). ~ GLEE
D83. East Indies (abr.). Work this out yourself. ~ EI


